Drain be-cpp's async queue when code outside any async block settles a promise, and let C++20 coroutines co_await Temper promises.
Upstream: not yet; logged for a Kotlin Temper fix (Kotlin's C++ fails async-broken-early and await-in-loop, note 520 in its functional-test-matrix.md); co_await to offer Kotlin Temper.

A promise completed or broken from a library's init, after `async_run` has
returned, queued its continuations with nothing left to run them. Settling
now drains the queue; inside a running task the drain is a no-op, as before.

Under C++20 (`__cpp_impl_coroutine`), `operator co_await` on a
`shared_ptr<Promise<R>>` suspends a C++ coroutine until the promise
settles, resuming it from the async queue. C++14 sees nothing new.

diff --git a/be-cpp/temper-core/promise.hpp b/be-cpp/temper-core/promise.hpp
index 7b6c25b..26d740d 100644
--- a/be-cpp/temper-core/promise.hpp
+++ b/be-cpp/temper-core/promise.hpp
@@ -6,6 +6,9 @@
 #include <vector>
 #include "temper_bubble.hpp"
 #include "generator.hpp"
+#if defined(__cpp_impl_coroutine)
+#include <coroutine>
+#endif
 
 namespace temper {
     namespace core {
@@ -60,12 +63,17 @@ namespace temper {
             bool ready = false;
             bool broken = false;
 
+            // Queues the continuations, and runs them unless a task that's
+            // running settled it: code outside any async block, such as a
+            // library's init completing a promise that a block awaits, has
+            // no loop of its own to drain the queue.
             void settle() {
                 std::vector<std::function<void()>> pending;
                 pending.swap(continuations);
                 for (std::function<void()>& task : pending) {
                     async_enqueue(std::move(task));
                 }
+                async_drain();
             }
         };
 
@@ -154,6 +162,31 @@ namespace temper {
             return promise->get();
         }
 
+#if defined(__cpp_impl_coroutine)
+        // C++20 interop: `co_await promise` in a C++ coroutine suspends it
+        // until the Temper promise settles, then gives its value, or throws
+        // `TemperBubble` if it's broken. The coroutine resumes from the async
+        // queue, as a Temper async block does.
+        template<class R>
+        struct PromiseAwaiter {
+            std::shared_ptr<Promise<R>> promise;
+            bool await_ready() const {
+                return promise->state->ready || promise->state->broken;
+            }
+            void await_suspend(std::coroutine_handle<> handle) {
+                promise->on_ready([handle]() { handle.resume(); });
+            }
+            R await_resume() {
+                return promise->get();
+            }
+        };
+
+        template<class R>
+        PromiseAwaiter<R> operator co_await(std::shared_ptr<Promise<R>> promise) {
+            return PromiseAwaiter<R>{std::move(promise)};
+        }
+#endif
+
